r easy to get  started they do a lot of the work for  you  um if you're a good programmer i don't  see anything wrong with like  i don't know like looking into  what they offer and how you can  customize it  in general i've always had more  fun and i've made more  headway using the open bci because it's  so customizable  but i also have a lot of knowledge about  all the different kinds of signals you  know  how to do ecg how to do stomach signals  how to do brain signals so i guess it's  like  if you don't have time for all of that  you can just throw on the emotive and  then you know you're recording brain  signals  so it's totally up to you  it's kind of like this like higher  customization  means more time lower customization  means less time but potentially less fun  so i hope that answers your question  oh thank you tim uh this  is super interesting yeah you're welcome  i  have a lot of fun doing this i don't  really get to talk to people  outside of my immediate realm of  research it's usually  you know only presenting to other people  that do eeg so i always have a lot of  fun  talking about this stuff and especially  now that companies like  neurolink are becoming mainstream and  black mirror is also popularizing the  dangerous effects of this kind of  technology i  also kind of want to show what's really  happening  when you're doing this kind of recording  and what the limitations are and to be  honest but to also be cautious  and be very clear that if you get into  this  you know you should be doing it for  yourself you should do it  if it's for someone else you need to  have their explicit consent  and also um you know  keep their data private or give them  full control over how the data is used  one question from the audience earlier  um was how does audio feedback to the  user influence the eeg  oh in this setup  i think in general in general  ooh this is such a broad question  that's really going to depend i can  imagine that there's some  uh there are some increases this is a  bit of literature i haven't looked at in  a while  um but i'd imagine that there's power  increases in the  like temporal lobe and like audio  processing centers  of the brain and besides that  audio feedback can just be like a cool  useful method to create a bci but  you know generally speaking you know i  can't really tell you like oh yeah if  you listen to a song  your brain signals are going to  synchronize with it  possibly um but i haven't looked at that  research in a while  so i think i saw another question what  is the biggest bottleneck right now  is it the hardware sensor or effective  signal processing  hmm  [Music]  that's a great question i personally  think  it's the sensor itself that the  way that we're recording signal right  now with these conductive materials  and also just using  electricity as our modality in general  is a huge limitation there needs to be  more innovation on the material science  side which is already happening  to make these signals easier to  record and also to reduce the signal to  noise ratio but most importantly  to figure out a recording method with  less artifacts  especially motion artifacts and  artifacts from  blinking like this is such a big problem  and  recording methods like what fmri uses  uh which is basically looking at change  in blood volume  get around this uh but the problem is  that they're extremely bulky and they're  not  portable so when we get to a point where  we can have  a portable fmri then i think we've  really revolutionized  like what we can do but right now the  form of that  is uh like i think the smallest form is  probably the size of a backpack  and the spatial resolution  is very shallow you know it's only like  a centimeter on the top of the brain  so you know like if we can get to a  point where we can have  the like recording mechanism uh with  less artifacts i think that's going to  be super important which like i think  maybe a lot of people would say oh  y